# Product-by-product operational walkthrough: one page per feature keeps every
# sequence readable as a stage rehearsal and a practical demo run sheet.
products=[
('01 / MineSentinel','Mining landscape intelligence','Use MineSentinel first to establish where the opportunity sits in the broader mining landscape. The source note describes a map, production context, concession visibility, GIS dashboards and exportable reports. Confirm coverage and update dates before presenting findings as complete or current.',[
('Interactive mining map','Purpose: locate the area and orient the room.','1. Start from a country or regional map. 2. Search or zoom to the district, mine or project area. 3. Turn on the relevant mine/site layer and inspect the visible context. 4. Select a site to open its available details. 5. Record the location and the next question for diligence.','The audience gets a common geographic frame quickly; analysts spend less time assembling separate map screenshots. A mapped point is a lead for verification, not proof of title or operational status.'),
('Concession visibility','Purpose: compare a project location with available concession boundaries.','1. Open the concession or allocation layer. 2. Search by identifier or navigate to the project coordinates. 3. Compare the project pin or uploaded area with displayed boundaries. 4. Note overlaps, gaps or missing records. 5. Export or capture the view for a follow-up with the official registry.','Makes spatial relationships easier to see and can expose questions earlier in screening. Boundary currency and authoritative status must be confirmed with the responsible source.'),
('Mineral production analytics','Purpose: understand available production context and trends.','1. Choose the relevant commodity, asset or reporting period exposed by the dashboard. 2. Inspect the displayed production measures and date range. 3. Compare records or trends available in the interface. 4. Note missing periods or definitions. 5. Carry the chart into the assessment with its source and date.','Reduces manual compilation and gives decision makers a consistent starting point. It does not establish reserves, forecast commodity prices or guarantee the figures are complete.'),
('GIS intelligence dashboard','Purpose: examine available spatial context in one analytical view.','1. Open the GIS dashboard for the selected geography. 2. Enable available context layers, such as remote-sensing or ground-telemetry inputs where supplied. 3. Compare the layers with the mine or concession context. 4. Mark areas that warrant a closer check. 5. Record which input and date support each observation.','Brings spatial evidence into one view and helps teams decide where to investigate. Availability of specific imagery or telemetry feeds varies; do not imply continuous monitoring without a confirmed integration.'),
('Exportable report','Purpose: turn a map or production view into a shareable briefing.','1. Select the geography and indicators to include. 2. Check labels, units, dates and source context. 3. Generate the available export (PDF, CSV or chart, depending on the build). 4. Review the export for omissions or stale data. 5. Share it with the next reviewer and capture their open questions.','Cuts repeat formatting work and creates a consistent handoff. A report is only as trustworthy as its underlying data coverage and the analyst’s verification.')]),
('02 / MineAsk','Evidence-backed site and concession assessment','Use MineAsk after MineSentinel establishes the location. It is described primarily as a query-and-assess workspace over available mining, geospatial, environmental, regulatory and market evidence. Capabilities vary in maturity; scheduled monitoring and alerts are partial or roadmap in the source note.',[
('Define the site or concession','Purpose: give the assessment an exact geographic subject.','1. Start with a permit/concession identifier, coordinates, map pin, district, route or uploaded boundary, depending on available inputs. 2. Confirm the name and geometry shown. 3. Set the assessment question and intended decision (screen, diligence or field plan). 4. Save the project with a clear owner and date.','Reduces ambiguity and repeated setup. A well-defined geography makes later evidence comparisons more useful.'),
('Join mining and geospatial context','Purpose: gather relevant evidence around the chosen place.','1. Select the layers available for the project: for example title/cadastre, geology, terrain, roads, water, infrastructure, settlements, protected areas or satellite context. 2. Inspect each layer’s source/date if exposed. 3. Compare the site boundary against surrounding context. 4. Flag missing or conflicting information. 5. Add specific follow-up checks.','Replaces repeated manual searches with a comparable view and makes dependencies easier to discuss. Coverage, freshness and source authority still need review.'),
('Ask a structured AI-assisted question','Purpose: turn an open-ended concern into a reviewable first-pass answer.','1. Ask a narrowly scoped question, such as “What location and access issues should we verify before fieldwork?” 2. Review the response and cited/linked evidence where available. 3. Separate sourced facts from interpretation. 4. Open the underlying map or record for consequential claims. 5. Assign unresolved items to a human reviewer.','Speeds initial synthesis and report preparation. The output is a screening aid, not legal advice, an official determination or a substitute for field judgment.'),
('Review a scorecard or screening view','Purpose: compare geological, legal, access, ESG and operational factors.','1. Select the relevant scorecard or screening dimensions supported in the build. 2. Inspect the factor-level evidence rather than relying only on an overall rating. 3. Identify hard stops, weak evidence and unknowns. 4. Compare candidate sites using the same criteria. 5. Decide whether to stop, investigate or proceed to specialist review.','Supports more consistent prioritization and can surface deal-breakers earlier. Any score is decision support; weights and data quality should be understood before use.'),
('Inspect licensing and regulatory context','Purpose: identify likely process steps and questions for formal confirmation.','1. Open the available licensing/regulatory context for the jurisdiction. 2. Review the stated process steps or conditions. 3. Check the source and version/date of the rule if displayed. 4. Build a checklist of documents or questions for counsel and the authority. 5. Record confirmation separately from the platform’s screening output.','Helps teams organize compliance work and spot likely gaps earlier. It does not monitor all regulatory changes in real time or replace official guidance/counsel.'),
('Build and hand off an evidence dossier','Purpose: send a useful, auditable first-pass assessment to the next team.','1. Choose the map, findings and open questions to include. 2. Preserve source lineage, confidence and version history where available. 3. Label items as evidence, inference or verification needed. 4. Export or share using features available in the deployed version. 5. Ask the receiving reviewer to resolve assigned items.','Improves handoffs, repeatability and reviewability. Collaboration, report automation and exports are described across project materials but maturity differs; demonstrate only confirmed functions.')]),
('03 / TerraPulse','Exploration evidence to prioritized targets','Use TerraPulse to help geologists prepare and explain a field or drilling decision. The source describes a proposed workflow that brings satellite, geophysical, geochemical and structural information together. Professional geological review and field validation remain essential.',[
('Create a project and define the decision','Purpose: focus analysis on a commodity, project area and next exploration choice.','1. Create a project for the concession or area of interest. 2. State the decision: regional screening, survey follow-up or target ranking. 3. Set the boundary and commodity/mineral-system context. 4. Invite the technical reviewers and apply appropriate client/project access.','Keeps analysis tied to a concrete decision and supports consistent team handover. Multi-client and role controls should be confirmed in the actual deployment.'),
('Ingest and harmonize data','Purpose: make supported survey and geospatial inputs comparable.','1. Add supported geophysical grids, geochemistry tables and satellite/geospatial files. 2. Check coordinate reference, units, coverage and dates. 3. Align the layers to the project area. 4. Review missing cells, offsets or format warnings. 5. Keep a record of transformations and inputs.','Reduces manual format conversion and spatial setup, freeing geologist time for interpretation. Unsupported formats, offline behavior and exact ingestion fidelity should be tested.'),
('Inspect unified layers','Purpose: compare evidence rather than relying on isolated static exports.','1. Open the unified project map. 2. Toggle one evidence layer at a time, then compare relevant combinations. 3. Inspect patterns against structural and geological context. 4. Mark areas for closer technical review. 5. Note where evidence conflicts or is absent.','Creates shared spatial context and can reduce rework between tools. A visual pattern is a hypothesis to investigate, not a discovery.'),
('Rank prospectivity or anomalies','Purpose: focus attention on areas with stronger combined evidence.','1. Select the available prospectivity/anomaly workflow and model settings. 2. Review ranked areas and the inputs behind each. 3. Compare top targets with weaker or contradictory signals. 4. Adjust priorities with geological judgment. 5. Select targets for validation, not automatic drilling.','Helps prioritize sampling, surveys and drilling budgets. Ranking quality depends on inputs and configured models; no discovery probability or resource declaration should be implied.'),
('Use the explainability matrix','Purpose: understand why a target is supported, weakened or uncertain.','1. Open a candidate target’s evidence matrix. 2. Review supporting and opposing factors. 3. Identify missing data and confidence limitations. 4. Ask a reviewer to challenge key assumptions. 5. Capture the rationale for advancing, pausing or rejecting the target.','Makes expert review possible and improves defensibility. It lowers the risk of blindly advancing a poorly supported target, but does not eliminate geological uncertainty.'),
('Package target outputs for field work','Purpose: move a reviewed target into the next practical workflow.','1. Select approved target polygons or points. 2. Create a map and target summary. 3. Export coordinates or files in a supported format. 4. Review coordinate reference and units before field use. 5. Attach a validation checklist and return field observations to the project.','Reduces manual report assembly and coordinate transfer and can speed mobilization. Verify export fidelity; resource/reserve reporting and formal 3D estimation remain separate workflows.')]),
('04 / MyMine AR Mine Digital Twin','Shared spatial review for mine teams','Use MyMine after the target and project have become an operating-site planning conversation. Its product note records owner-stated AR twin, streaming, multi-user, multi-device and hybrid edge-sync capabilities; the source explicitly says deployment maturity and specifications still need verification.',[
('Open the mine twin','Purpose: give reviewers a common three-dimensional view of the site.','1. Load the available 3D mine model into the session. 2. Confirm which survey/CAD/GIS source and date the model represents. 3. Orient the group to a recognizable pit, access or infrastructure landmark. 4. State the model’s known accuracy and update limits. 5. Keep the twin framed as a decision-support view.','Makes spatial relationships easier to discuss than separate 2D screenshots. Model build/update sources and accuracy are assumptions to confirm; the twin is not a certified planning or safety system.'),
('Join on multiple devices','Purpose: let on-site and remote participants see the same context.','1. Start a shared session. 2. Invite the mine planner, geotech, HSE or maintenance reviewer. 3. Connect using the supported headset, tablet, phone or web client. 4. Confirm participants see the same location and annotations. 5. Capture decisions and follow-up owners using the verified collaboration controls.','Reduces “which plan are you looking at?” friction and supports remote participation. Supported models, concurrency and feature parity must be validated.'),
('Review streamed information','Purpose: view live or refreshed information in spatial context.','1. Connect only a confirmed data source. 2. Display its timestamp, units and source label. 3. Compare the incoming state with the model and the question under review. 4. Note latency, missing intervals or stale values. 5. Use the displayed information to inform discussion, not to issue automated control actions.','Can bring data into a shared review and help teams understand context. Source integrations, latency, volume and operational reliability are unspecified; this is not SCADA or fleet dispatch.'),
('Use edge sync for weak connectivity','Purpose: maintain a usable session at a remote or poorly connected site.','1. Confirm the site edge node and power/network setup with IT/OT. 2. Start the session while connected and verify the local model/data cache. 3. Test the intended offline period in a controlled pilot. 4. Reconnect and inspect synchronization and any conflicts. 5. Record data loss, delay and recovery behavior before operational reliance.','May support continuity at sites with unreliable connectivity. Offline duration, conflict handling, hardware and sync reliability are unverified; do not promise an eight-hour offline shift until tested.'),
('Run a collaborative planning review','Purpose: align a cross-functional group around one spatial question.','1. State one question, such as “where does the proposed access route meet the area under review?” 2. Navigate to that area together. 3. Let each role describe evidence and constraints. 4. Record agreed actions and unknowns in the approved system of record. 5. End with owners and follow-up dates.','Can improve shared understanding and meeting clarity. Plan-vs-actual overlays, grade, geofence, ground movement, remote assist and safety outcomes are listed as unverified; use only features demonstrated and validated.')])]
story += [PageBreak(),band('Feature-by-feature product walkthroughs','A practical sequence for demonstrating what each product does, how a user moves through it, what the feature produces and why that step matters.','Presenter run sheet'),Spacer(1,10),P('These pages deepen the product portions of the three story flows. Treat each numbered sequence as a demo script: state the user’s question, follow the steps, show the output, then name the benefit and limitation. “Described” does not guarantee that a feature is deployed; validate the live build before the presentation.'),PageBreak()]
for ptitle,subtitle,intro,features in products:
    story += [band(ptitle,subtitle,'Software walkthrough'),Spacer(1,9),card('Use this software when','Role in the end-to-end story',intro,PALE),PageBreak()]
    for fname,purpose,steps,benefit in features:
        story += [P(ptitle.upper()+'  /  FEATURE WALKTHROUGH','Label'),P(fname,'H1x'),HRFlowable(width='100%',thickness=1,color=GOLD,spaceAfter=10),card('User goal','What the user is trying to do',purpose,SAND),Spacer(1,9),card('Step by step','Demonstration sequence',[P(steps.replace('1.','<br/><b>1.</b>').replace('2.','<br/><b>2.</b>').replace('3.','<br/><b>3.</b>').replace('4.','<br/><b>4.</b>').replace('5.','<br/><b>5.</b>'))]),Spacer(1,9),card('Result and benefit','What changes for the user',benefit,PALE),PageBreak()]
story += [band('How to use the feature walkthroughs on stage','Choose only the steps needed to make the product’s role clear. Keep each software moment tied to the same mine, decision and evidence chain.','Demo guidance'),Spacer(1,12),card('A reliable demo rhythm','Question → steps → output → benefit → boundary','Open with the decision the user needs to make. Show the steps in sequence. Pause on the output. Explain the practical benefit. State any data, integration or maturity limit that changes how the audience should interpret it.',SAND),Spacer(1,9),P('This rhythm keeps the presentation technically grounded while still showing how the four products connect from sector context to site-level collaboration. Use the presenter’s actual build for screenshots and live actions; where a feature is not present, keep the walkthrough at concept level and identify the required integration or validation.'),PageBreak()]
doc.build(story,onFirstPage=footer,onLaterPages=footer)
print(OUT)
